    "['clock extend-ssm-control [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ntp extended-ssm-control enable', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ntp extended-ssm-control enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ntp extended-ssm-control enable\\n##\\n\\nNote: While Juniper's `extended-ssm-control` under NTP protocols is the closest functional equivalent to Huawei's `clock extend-ssm-control`, please be aware that the exact implementation might differ slightly as Huawei's command operates at the clock synchronization level while Juniper implements SSM control within NTP context. The actual behavior should be verified in your specific network environment.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ntp extended-ssm-control enable'}]]": 1,
    "['clock bits-type [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set chassis synchronization interfaces bits 0 speed 2mbps', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set chassis synchronization interfaces bits 0 speed 2mbps', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset chassis synchronization interfaces bits 0 speed 2mbps\\n##', 'trans_template': 'set chassis synchronization interfaces bits 0 speed 2mbps'}]]": 1,
    "['clock source [parameter1] clock-id [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ntp interface bits0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ntp interface bits0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI command `clock source bits0 clock-id 1`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ntp interface bits0\\nset protocols ntp reference-clock 1 interface bits0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper typically uses NTP for clock synchronization, and `bits0` would be the physical interface name for the external BITS input.\\n2. The `reference-clock` statement assigns the clock ID (1 in this case) to the BITS interface.\\n3. The exact implementation might vary slightly depending on Juniper platform and OS version. Some platforms may use `chassis synchronization` commands instead of NTP for BITS configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ntp interface bits0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ntp reference-clock 1 interface bits0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ntp reference-clock 1 interface bits0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI command `clock source bits0 clock-id 1`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ntp interface bits0\\nset protocols ntp reference-clock 1 interface bits0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper typically uses NTP for clock synchronization, and `bits0` would be the physical interface name for the external BITS input.\\n2. The `reference-clock` statement assigns the clock ID (1 in this case) to the BITS interface.\\n3. The exact implementation might vary slightly depending on Juniper platform and OS version. Some platforms may use `chassis synchronization` commands instead of NTP for BITS configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ntp reference-clock 1 interface bits0'}]]": 1,
