    "['clock synchronization enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ntp interface ge-1/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols ntp interface ge-1/0/0\\n##\\n\\n(Note: Juniper doesn\\'t have an exact 1:1 \"clock synchronization enable\" command. The closest equivalent is enabling NTP on the interface, which achieves similar time synchronization functionality. The Huawei command enables synchronization clock functionality on the interface, while Juniper\\'s approach is to configure NTP at the interface level.)', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ntp interface ge-1/0/0'}]]": 1,
    "['clock clock-id [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ptp interface ge-1/0/0 clock-identifier 2', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Huawei `clock clock-id 2` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ptp interface ge-1/0/0 clock-identifier 2\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper typically uses PTP (Precision Time Protocol) for clock synchronization configurations. The command sets the clock identifier (2 in this case) for the specified interface (ge-1/0/0). The interface naming convention may vary slightly depending on the Juniper platform (e.g., might be ge-0/0/0 instead of ge-1/0/0).\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ptp interface ge-1/0/0 clock-identifier 2'}]]": 1,
    "['clock synchronization enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols synchronization interfaces ge-1/0/0 enable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols synchronization interfaces ge-1/0/0 enable\\nset protocols synchronization interfaces ge-2/0/0 enable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The HUAWEI `clock synchronization enable` command enables clock synchronization on an interface.\\n- In Juniper, the equivalent is configuring synchronization under `protocols synchronization` for the specific interface (`ge-1/0/0` and `ge-2/0/0` in this case, matching HUAWEI's GigabitEthernet interfaces).\\n- The `enable` keyword activates the feature, mirroring HUAWEI's intent.\\n\\nNote: Juniper uses `ge-x/y/z` interface naming (instead of GigabitEthernet) and places clock synchronization under the `protocols` hierarchy.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols synchronization interfaces ge-1/0/0 enable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols synchronization interfaces ge-2/0/0 enable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols synchronization interfaces ge-1/0/0 enable\\nset protocols synchronization interfaces ge-2/0/0 enable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The HUAWEI `clock synchronization enable` command enables clock synchronization on an interface.\\n- In Juniper, the equivalent is configuring synchronization under `protocols synchronization` for the specific interface (`ge-1/0/0` and `ge-2/0/0` in this case, matching HUAWEI's GigabitEthernet interfaces).\\n- The `enable` keyword activates the feature, mirroring HUAWEI's intent.\\n\\nNote: Juniper uses `ge-x/y/z` interface naming (instead of GigabitEthernet) and places clock synchronization under the `protocols` hierarchy.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols synchronization interfaces ge-2/0/0 enable'}]]": 1,
